    "result_text": "All imports are still used. The implementation is complete and the test suite is green.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the `bulkSignupWarning` rule for `POST /api/owners`: when more than 80 owners have already been created for the new owner's registration day, the created (and later read-back) owner reports `bulkSignupWarning: true`, otherwise `false`.\n\nFollowing the existing `namesakeCount` pattern (a count computed at creation, persisted on the entity, and returned via the mapper \u2014 including on `GET /api/owners/{id}`, which the acceptance test exercises):\n\n1. **`Owner` model** \u2014 added a persisted `Boolean bulkSignupWarning` field (`bulk_signup_warning` column) with getter/setter.\n2. **Schemas** \u2014 added the `bulk_signup_warning` column to all four DB schemas (hsqldb, h2, mysql, postgres).\n3. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added the read-only `bulkSignupWarning` boolean to the `Owner` schema so the generated `OwnerDto` carries it and MapStruct auto-maps it (no mapper change needed).\n4. **`OwnerRepository.countRegisteredOn(LocalDate)`** \u2014 extracted the \"owners created on a day\" count as a single reusable default method, and refactored `EnsureDailyOwnerCapacity` to use it instead of its inline stream, so the daily-cap rule and the new warning share one definition.\n5. **`AssignOwnerBulkSignupWarning`** \u2014 a new single-responsibility function step that sets the flag to `count > 80`, running after `assignNamesakeCount` (before save, in the transaction) so the count excludes the not-yet-persisted owner.\n6. **`owners.POST.yml`** \u2014 wired the new step into the pipeline.\n\n`AcceptanceTest` (the only test class) and the full suite build green.",
